WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.640
Why don't siblings look exactly alike, even with the same two parents?

2
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.200
Every child gets half of their chromosomes from each parent.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.600
Yet brothers and sisters almost never get the same mix.

4
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.680
The answer is in how eggs and sperm are made.

5
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:28.213
Most human body cells have 46 chromosomes, in 23 pairs.

6
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:33.613
You got one chromosome of each pair from your mother and one from your father.

7
00:00:34.693 --> 00:00:37.893
A cell with two sets of chromosomes is diploid.

8
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:40.553
A cell with one set is haploid.

9
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:47.273
In humans, a gamete, an egg or a sperm, is haploid, with 23 chromosomes.

10
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:53.417
Meiosis is cell division that makes haploid cells from a diploid cell.

11
00:00:54.477 --> 00:00:57.317
First, the cell copies its DNA once.

12
00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:02.497
Each chromosome is now two identical copies, called sister chromatids.

13
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:05.237
Then the cell divides twice.

14
00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:10.117
In the first division, the two chromosomes of each pair are pulled apart.

15
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.417
In the second, the sister chromatids separate.

16
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:15.897
Usually, four cells form.

17
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.000
Meiosis mixes alleles in two ways.

18
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.080
An allele is one version of a gene.

19
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.120
The first way is crossing over.

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.440
In the first division, the two chromosomes of a pair lie side by side and swap

21
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:38.620
matching pieces. After a swap, one chromosome in your sex cell can carry alleles

22
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.620
from both your mother and your father.

23
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:44.403
The second way is independent assortment.

24
00:01:45.223 --> 00:01:48.083
Each pair lines up and separates on its own.

25
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:51.663
A sex cell gets one chromosome from each pair.

26
00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:56.943
Each pair can send either chromosome, so every pair doubles the number of

27
00:01:56.943 --> 00:02:02.263
combinations. Three pairs give 2 × 2 × 2, which is 8.

28
00:02:02.263 --> 00:02:09.743
Humans have 23 pairs, so one parent can make 2²³ combinations, about

29
00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:11.083
8.4 million.

30
00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:15.873
Fertilization is when an egg and a sperm join.

31
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:21.533
Their two sets of 23 make a diploid cell with 46 again.

32
00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:26.213
Each sibling grows from a different egg and a different sperm.

33
00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.393
Each of those sex cells is a new mix.

34
00:02:30.453 --> 00:02:34.733
Meiosis halves the chromosomes and shuffles them, so siblings differ.
